const CARDS=[
C('c0','c1','Price elasticity','ε = %Δq / %Δp (usually negative)\nelastic |ε| > 1, inelastic |ε| < 1'),C('c1','c1','Four conditions of perfect competition','market atomism · perfect information · homogeneity · free entry and exit'),
C('c2','c1','Market power (definition)','the ability to substantially influence market prices: p*(q*) > MC(q*), markup µ > 1'),C('c3','c1','Lerner index','(p* − MC(q*)) / p* = 1/|ε|: elasticity and market power are inversely related'),
C('c4','c1','Four barriers to entry','technological (economies of scale) · exclusive rights and legal barriers · strategic behaviour · network externalities'),
C('c5','c1','Monopolistic competition','many firms, differentiated products, free entry/exit, perfect information; own downward-sloping demand; zero profit in the long run'),
C('c6','c1','Effect of entry on a monopolistic competitor’s demand','shifts inward and becomes flatter (more elastic)'),C('c7','c1','Oligopoly vs other forms','only in oligopoly must firms take rivals’ behaviour into account (strategic interaction)'),
C('c8','c1','Is market power bad?','static inefficiency, excessive power destroys welfare and lowers the labour share; but differentiation is valued and market power can stimulate innovation (dynamic efficiency)'),
C('c9','c1','Pareto efficiency','no agent can be made better off without making another worse off; perfect competition maximizes static welfare (CS + PS)'),
C('c10','c2','Dominant strategy','the strategy that gives the best result whatever the other player does'),C('c11','c2','Nash equilibrium','a combination of strategies where no player wishes to change his strategy given the expected strategy of the others'),
C('c12','c2','Equilibrium in dominant strategies','an expected solution consisting of dominant strategies; always a NE (not the converse)'),C('c13','c2','Expected solution','a unique Nash equilibrium; with several NE, none without coordination'),
C('c14','c2','Prisoner’s dilemma insight','the blind pursuit of self-interest does not necessarily lead to the best outcome for the players'),C('c15','c2','Cartel agreement','firms coordinate output and/or price to maximize the sum of profits, as one merged monopoly'),
C('c16','c2','Battle of the sexes','no dominant strategies, two NE: coordination, sequential choice and first-mover advantage matter'),C('c17','c2','Mechanism design','“If you can’t win the game, change the rules”: change payoffs (CO₂ taxes) so the desired outcome becomes an equilibrium'),
C('c18','c2','Tit-for-tat','begin by maintaining; maintain if the rival maintained last period; break if the rival broke'),C('c19','c2','Finite vs indefinite repetition','known last period: cooperation unravels backwards; unknown end: retaliation can sustain cooperation'),
C('c20','c2','Backward induction','solve a sequential game from the last decision to the first'),C('c21','c2','SPNE','complete strategy profile from backward induction, including actions at unreached nodes'),
C('c22','c2','Credible threat','carrying it out is optimal when the node is reached'),C('c23','c2','Centipede game result','A stops immediately (2; 0) instead of (8; 6): strategic foresight'),
C('c24','c2','Ultimatum game','theory (9; 1); in practice fairer offers and rejections: fairness belongs in the payoffs'),C('c25','c2','Paradox of Bertrand','homogeneous duopoly + price competition → p = MC = min AC, zero profit'),
C('c26','c2','Hotelling, fixed prices','both carts at the centre: homogeneous duopoly, unique NE (not socially optimal: 0.5 km vs 250 m)'),C('c27','c2','Hotelling, free prices','centre no longer NE: firms differentiate to escape Bertrand; more price competition → more differentiation'),
C('c28','c3','MR of p = a − bq','MR = a − 2bq'),C('c29','c3','Profit maximization rule','MR(q*) = MC(q*), then check p* ≥ AC (long run) or p* ≥ AVC (short run)'),
C('c30','c3','MC and AC','MC cuts AC (and AVC) at their minimum'),C('c31','c3','Returns to scale and LAC','increasing: LAC falls, LMC < LAC · constant: LAC = LMC · decreasing: LAC rises, LMC > LAC'),
C('c32','c3','Economic vs accounting profit','economic profit also deducts implicit opportunity costs'),C('c33','c3','First-degree price discrimination','price = WTP of each buyer; CS = 0; efficient quantity; doubtful on distribution'),
C('c34','c3','Market segmentation rule','MRi = MC in each segment; more elastic segment → lower price; no resale possible'),C('c35','c3','Uniform-price demand of two segments','horizontal sum q(p) = q1(p) + q2(p), where both buy'),
C('c36','c3','Monopolistic competition, long run','demand tangent to AC: MR = MC and p = AC, zero profit, p > MC, q below qSE'),
C('c37','c4','Price taker','p = AR = MR; produce where p = MC; supply curve = MC above min AC'),C('c38','c4','Market equilibrium with free entry','p* = MC(q*) = AC(q*), q* = qSE, p* = min AC, n* = QD(p*)/q*'),
C('c39','c4','Entry after positive profit','supply shifts right, price falls, output per firm falls, firms and total output rise'),C('c40','c4','Natural monopoly','qSE large relative to demand at min AC: a second firm’s residual demand cannot cover AC'),
C('c41','c4','Demand shock under perfect competition','output fixed, price falls until buyers take it, possibly below AC'),C('c42','c4','Cost shock','MC and AC up, supply left: higher price, lower quantity, exit if losses persist'),
C('c43','c4','Static vs dynamic efficiency','p = MC (static) vs rewards for innovation (dynamic): aim for effective competition')];

